- op: create  # 4e40
  at: '2026-05-26T08:13:21Z'  # 4e40
  by: agent  # 4e40
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 4e40
  clock: 1  # 4e40
  nonce: '4e40'  # 4e40
  data:  # 4e40
    kind: invariant  # 4e40
    title: "Framework detection must promote frameworks from prod-non-test import edges, not just manifest declarations"  # 4e40
    status: violated  # 4e40
    priority: 3  # 4e40
    parent:  # 4e40
    tags:  # 4e40
    - framework_detection  # 4e40
    - profile  # 4e40
    - analysis_quality  # 4e40
    - structural  # 4e40
    isbefore: []  # 4e40
    duplicate_of: []  # 4e40
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 4e40
    pr_ref:  # 4e40
    description: "## Statement\n\nFramework detection should consider BOTH manifest declarations AND prod-non-test import-edge evidence as positive signals. A repo whose code imports a framework's package — and is not test-only — should have that framework in `profile.frameworks` (production), regardless of whether the local package.json / pyproject.toml / Gemfile / Cargo.toml declares the dep. The current implementation only flows imports → manifest as a *demotion* check (manifest detects, no prod imports → `dev_frameworks`); it never flows imports → manifest as a *promotion* (manifest silent, prod imports → `frameworks`).\n\n## Root cause\n\n`profile.py::refine_frameworks` already builds `module_importers: dict[str, list[str]]` at lines 3038-3059, classifies importers via `is_test_file` at line 3093, and uses this data structure to make the demote decision at lines 3088-3102. But the function's loop iterates `for fw in profile.frameworks:` — it only ever considers frameworks already detected from manifests. Frameworks reached only by imports stay invisible. The data plumbing exists; the policy doesn't.\n\nDownstream cost: `enrich_symbols` loads framework YAML patterns only for `profile.frameworks` (not `dev_frameworks`). When a framework isn't detected, its YAML never loads, and any pattern with `usage.kind: ^call$` that targets that framework's call-site names silently doesn't fire — even though analyzers correctly emit the UCs.\n\n## Prior occurrences (this gap recurs across languages and ecosystems)\n\nEach of the items below patched a different surface of the same underlying problem with a different escape hatch:\n\n| WI | Surface | Ecosystem | Fix shape | Outcome |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **WI-lohok** (2026-04-26, P2 done) | Rails autoload-by-convention defeats `refine_frameworks` import-edge confirmation; chatwoot reported 0 controller/route/form/serializer concept hits. | Ruby / Bundler | `_AUTOLOAD_BY_CONVENTION_FRAMEWORKS` exemption frozenset (`profile.py:369`) — skip demotion entirely for autoload conventions. | Targeted; the exemption set grows per language as new autoload frameworks land. |\n| **WI-himas** (2026-05-19, P3 done) | Manifest scan misses `-r requirements/base.txt` chains; bakerydemo + many Django apps reported `frameworks=[]`. | Python / pip-r-chains | Smarter manifest scanner (follow `-r` includes recursively). | Targeted; each new manifest composition convention (Poetry groups, Pipenv categories, etc.) needs its own scanner update. |\n| **WI-rofiz** (commit `83310309fa`) | Bare `graphql` npm package was a false-positive trigger; many repos install it for typedefs / codegen without running a GraphQL server. | Node / npm | Tighten manifest signal — drop FP-prone bare packages from the framework's dependency-name list. | Targeted; tightens accuracy but doesn't help workspace-import cases. |\n| **WI-donud** (2026-05-26, P3 done — this session) | apollo-server `smoke-test/` consumer imports Apollo via workspace; local package.json declares only graphql + make-fetch-happen; `frameworks=[]`; `graphql.yaml` never loads; `startStandaloneServer` UC has no pattern to match; routes.txt = 0. | Node / npm workspace | YAML relocation — move high-specificity Apollo patterns from `graphql.yaml` (framework-gated) to `node-http.yaml` (always-loaded). | Targeted; works because Apollo names are high-specificity (no realistic collisions). Doesn't generalize to lower-specificity framework patterns. |\n\nAspirational signal: `profile.py:341` has `\"swiftui\": [\"swiftui\"],  # Detected via imports, not SPM`. The comment claims import-based detection; the implementation at `_detect_swift_frameworks` (line 2000) just parses Package.swift like everything else. The system has anticipated this gap for at least one framework but never built the detection path.\n\n## Why a structural fix now\n\nFour targeted patches in five weeks across four ecosystems (Ruby, Python, Node/manifest, Node/workspace). Each new ecosystem with a different composition convention costs another patch. The data plumbing for a structural fix already exists in `refine_frameworks`. The marginal cost of one positional promotion phase is < 100 LOC and unblocks future cases automatically.\n\n## Proposed mechanism\n\nExtend `refine_frameworks` with a second phase after the existing demote phase:\n\n1. **Promote phase.** For every framework F NOT currently in `profile.frameworks` AND NOT currently in `profile.dev_frameworks`:\n   - Look up F's import-module patterns (the existing `_import_modules_for_framework(fw)` helper).\n   - Check whether any prod-non-test source file imports a module matching one of those patterns (the existing `module_importers` data structure + the existing `is_test_file` gate).\n   - If yes, promote F to `profile.frameworks`.\n2. **Conflict resolution with autoload exemption.** A framework in `_AUTOLOAD_BY_CONVENTION_FRAMEWORKS` that's already in `profile.frameworks` from manifest detection skips the demote check (existing behavior, line 3075). The new promote check naturally handles autoload-only cases: if Rails isn't in the Gemfile (rare but possible for fork-from-template repos), import edges to `rails` from non-test files would still promote it.\n3. **Specificity gate (optional).** For the promote path, optionally restrict to frameworks whose import-module patterns are \"specific enough\" — e.g., scoped npm packages (`@apollo/server`) or compound paths (`@apollo/server/standalone`) get promotion; bare names (`react`, `vue`) require manifest evidence. Mirrors WI-rofiz's \"bare graphql is too weak\" lesson on a different axis.\n\n## Design tensions to resolve in the WI\n\n1. **Direction of inference.** Manifest→imports (current, demote-on-mismatch) vs imports→manifest (proposed, promote-when-silent) vs both. Currently they compose cleanly because the demote runs once at the end; the promote would have to run in a defined order relative to the demote (probably promote-then-demote, so a freshly-promoted framework is still subject to test-only-import demotion).\n2. **False positive cost.** `import { startStandaloneServer } from '@apollo/server'` is a strong positive signal. But `// import { foo } from 'rails'` in a comment is not — INV-vunaf taught us that substring-matching manifests is dangerous; the analyzer-side `imports` edges should NOT have this problem (they're AST-parsed, not text-matched), but the WI should verify on the cohort that the imports edges don't include commented or stringified imports.\n3. **Test/fixture noise.** Test files often import frameworks the production app doesn't use (apollo-server's own integration-testsuite imports Apollo extensively but is itself a test fixture, not a production deployment). The existing `is_test_file` filter handles this; the promote path must use the same gate.\n4. **Specificity gate calibration.** What's \"specific enough\"? `@apollo/server` and `@apollo/server/standalone` are clear yes. `react` is clear no (used in non-React build tooling). `rocket` (Rust)? `flask` (Python)? Probably each language ecosystem needs its own line. Could draw the line at \"if the framework's `_import_modules_for_framework` patterns are all scoped or path-compound, allow promotion; if any bare name is in the set, require manifest evidence.\"\n\n## Spec / ADR compatibility\n\n- **`docs/hypergumbo-spec.md` §profile (line 758)** describes the shape of `profile` but does NOT constrain detection methodology. No spec change required.\n- **No ADR** governs framework detection methodology directly. ADR-0022 (Language Profile Registry) is adjacent — it consolidates per-language YAML config — but the registry is explicitly deferred (\"Status: Proposed — by-language LanguageProfile runtime aggregation deferred pending consumer demand\"). The structural fix doesn't depend on or block ADR-0022.\n\n## Closure criterion\n\nThis INV closes `satisfied` when:\n\n1. `refine_frameworks` (or a successor) promotes a framework to `profile.frameworks` when prod-non-test imports exist, even if the manifest is silent.\n2. The promote path is gated on the specificity rule (calibrated per the WI's design discussion) to avoid false positives from generic import names.\n3. A bakeoff cohort confirms the four prior surfaces are now silently caught by the structural path:\n   - apollo-server/smoke-test/ → graphql + apollo framework detected, route count ≥ 1 (regression check: same as WI-donud's targeted fix).\n   - bakerydemo (or similar Django-with-layered-requirements repo) → django framework detected from imports even if requirements.txt is empty.\n   - chatwoot (or similar Bundler-autoload Rails app) → rails framework detected from imports even when refine_frameworks would have demoted it.\n   - A Node monorepo where a thin subdir imports Apollo without local package.json declaration → apollo framework detected.\n4. INV-vunaf-style false-positive audit on the chosen specificity gate: hypergumbo self-analysis should NOT promote any frameworks the repo doesn't actually use.\n\n## Suggested WI split\n\nSingle WI is plausible (estimated <500 LOC + bakeoff). If split:\n\n- **WI-promote-phase** — Add the promote phase to `refine_frameworks` with the specificity gate. Bakeoff cohort.\n- **WI-deprecate-targeted-patches** — Once the structural fix is validated, audit the four targeted patches (WI-lohok exemption, WI-himas `-r` scanner, WI-rofiz bare-name guard, WI-donud YAML relocation) and decide which can be reverted / generalized into the structural path. This is a follow-on after the structural fix lands and the bakeoff confirms no regression.\n\n## Related (non-blocking)\n\n- **INV-vunaf** (satisfied) — Manifest-substring detection false positives. Adjacent: this INV is about a different signal source entirely (imports), but the FP-prevention lessons apply.\n- **WI-jagus-bufip** (todo_hard P3) — Intra-file bare-name reads. Unrelated to framework detection but shares the \"analyzer side ships UCs, downstream consumer doesn't see them end-to-end\" failure mode (different downstream consumer)."  # 4e40
    fields:  # 4e40
      statement: "A framework should be reported in profile.frameworks when prod-non-test source code imports modules matching that framework's import-module patterns, regardless of whether the local manifest declares the dep. The current refine_frameworks pipeline uses import edges only for demotion (manifest detected, no imports → dev_frameworks), never for promotion (manifest silent, prod imports → frameworks)."  # 4e40
      root_cause: "profile.py::refine_frameworks already builds module_importers from import edges and classifies importers via is_test_file, but iterates only over frameworks already detected from manifests — frameworks reached only by imports stay invisible. Workspace-imported deps (apollo-server smoke-test), autoload-by-convention (Rails on chatwoot), layered manifests (Django -r requirements/base.txt), and bare-name FP guards (graphql npm) have each been patched in isolation; the data plumbing for a structural fix has existed since refine_frameworks landed."  # 4e40
- op: discuss  # 0dc6
